picolibc 1.8.12's <sys/types.h> does not pull in <sys/select.h>. glibc
and the BSDs both do, and a great deal of portable code relies on it:
fd_set, FD_SET and select() used having included only <sys/types.h>.
CPython's select module is one such program -- without this it reports
select as implicitly declared and `struct timeval` as an incomplete
type.

Not specific to m68k.

diff -ru a/libc/include/sys/types.h b/libc/include/sys/types.h
--- a/libc/include/sys/types.h	2026-09-22 15:36:22.499516374 -0600
+++ b/libc/include/sys/types.h	2026-09-22 15:36:32.659211810 -0600
@@ -257,4 +257,15 @@
 #define __Long  int32_t
 #define __ULong uint32_t
 
+/*
+ * <sys/select.h>, as glibc and the BSDs do from here. A great deal of
+ * portable code uses fd_set, FD_SET and select() having included only
+ * <sys/types.h>, because on those systems that is enough -- CPython's
+ * select module is one. Without it the program does not see select at
+ * all, and `struct timeval` is an incomplete type in the middle of it.
+ */
+#if __BSD_VISIBLE || __POSIX_VISIBLE || __XSI_VISIBLE
+#include <sys/select.h>
+#endif
+
 #endif /* _SYS_TYPES_H */
